# --- linux-libc-dev (6.12.100-1+dhi0); kernel UAPI headers only, no running
# kernel code in this image; no Debian fix on the trixie track ---
# Confirmed via the PR #547 Container Security run against
# ghcr.io/byronwilliamscpa/dhi-python:3.14-debian13
# (workflow run 30733734393): this is the image's only remaining finding
# (Total: 1, HIGH: 1, CRITICAL: 0), reported with an empty Fixed Version and
# status "affected". Independently corroborated against the Debian security
# tracker on 2026-08-02: trixie (6.12.94-1) and trixie-security (6.12.100-1)
# both record "vulnerable"; the fix landed only in sid (7.0.12-1), which this
# base image does not track. The defect is an AMD GPU DRM fix in
# amdgpu_hmm_range_get_pages, hardware this deployment does not use at all.
# Same kernel-UAPI-headers non-applicability rationale as the blocks above:
# compile-time headers, not a running kernel binary, and the container runs on
# the host's kernel. Documented in docs/known-vulnerabilities.md; reassess
# 2026-09-30.
CVE-2026-63879

# linux-libc-dev newly-disclosed CVE, confirmed via the PR #597 Container
# Security run against ghcr.io/byronwilliamscpa/dhi-python:3.14-debian13
# (workflow run 30950314291, job 92130520302): empty Fixed Version, status
# "affected", and the run's only finding (Total: 1, HIGH: 1, CRITICAL: 0). The
# base-image version is unchanged at 6.12.100-1+dhi0, and the run on main 3.5
# hours earlier was clean, so this is a Trivy feed refresh rather than anything
# that PR introduced. Independently corroborated against the Debian security
# tracker on 2026-08-04: trixie (6.12.94-1) and trixie-security (6.12.100-1)
# both record "vulnerable"; the fix landed only in sid (7.1.6-1), which this
# base image does not track. The defect is in KVM's x86 shadow-paging MMU,
# reachable only by a host process driving /dev/kvm ioctls; this image contains
# no hypervisor and requests no KVM device access. Same kernel-UAPI-headers
# non-applicability rationale as the blocks above: compile-time headers, not a
# running kernel binary, and the container runs on the host's kernel.
# Documented in docs/known-vulnerabilities.md; reassess 2026-09-30.
CVE-2026-64561

# linux-libc-dev newly-disclosed CVE, confirmed via the PR #644 Container
# Security run against ghcr.io/byronwilliamscpa/dhi-python:3.14-debian13
# (workflow run 31262431949): empty Fixed Version, status "affected", and the
# run's only finding (Total: 1, HIGH: 1, CRITICAL: 0). The base-image version is
# unchanged at 6.12.100-1+dhi0 and the pinned digest is byte-identical on main
# and the PR head (sha256:f4a77e21...), while the last run that actually
# executed this job (30973596262, 2026-08-05) was clean, so this is a Trivy feed
# refresh rather than anything that PR introduced. Note that main's own tip
# shows this job "skipped", because container-security.yml skips on
# chore(release): tips; a green main is not evidence either way here.
# Independently corroborated against the Debian security tracker on 2026-08-08:
# trixie (6.12.94-1) and trixie-security (6.12.101-1) both record "vulnerable",
# as do bookworm and forky (7.1.6-1); the fix landed only in sid (7.1.7-1),
# which this base image does not track. The defect is in the x86 SRSO Safe-RET
# speculative-execution mitigation, which is running-kernel CPU-mitigation code
# whose exploitation requires injecting interrupts on the host. Same
# kernel-UAPI-headers non-applicability rationale as the blocks above:
# compile-time headers, not a running kernel binary, and the container runs on
# the host's kernel. Documented in docs/known-vulnerabilities.md; reassess
# 2026-09-30.
CVE-2026-68480

# linux-libc-dev newly-disclosed CVE, confirmed via the PR #644 Container
# Security run against ghcr.io/byronwilliamscpa/dhi-python:3.14-debian13
# (workflow run 31262431949): empty Fixed Version, status "affected", and the
# run's only finding (Total: 1, HIGH: 1, CRITICAL: 0). The base-image digest is
# unchanged at sha256:f4a77e21fb25b71ccb183a25a53a1e87db2a3c11b422941b25708c2b5f3b1b13
# (linux-libc-dev 6.12.100-1+dhi0) and is byte-identical on main and the PR head,
# and the last run that actually executed this workflow (30973596262, 2026-08-05)
# was clean, so this is a Trivy feed refresh rather than anything PR #644
# introduced: that PR changes three lockfiles and no Dockerfile. Note main's own
# tip reports this job as skipped, because container-security.yml skips on
# `chore(release):` tips, so a green main is not evidence either way here.
# Independently corroborated against the Debian security tracker on 2026-08-08:
# trixie (6.12.94-1) and trixie-security (6.12.101-1) both record "vulnerable",
# as do bookworm, bullseye, and forky (7.1.6-1); the fix landed only in sid
# (7.1.7-1), which this base image does not track. The defect is in the x86
# Safe-RET speculative-execution mitigation for AMD SRSO hardware, reachable only
# by an attacker able to inject interrupts into a running kernel's mitigation
# sequence. Same kernel-UAPI-headers non-applicability rationale as the blocks
# above: compile-time headers, not a running kernel binary, and the container
# runs on the host's kernel, whose mitigation state this image cannot influence
# or patch. Documented in docs/known-vulnerabilities.md; reassess 2026-09-30.
CVE-2026-68480

# Nine linux-libc-dev kernel-header CVEs surfaced by the PR #709 Container
# Security run (Trivy v0.70.0, workflow run 31760080363): all High, all with an
# empty Fixed Version and status "affected", and the only findings in the whole
# image (every Python package scanned reports 0).
#
# Feed refresh, not a PR regression. PR #709 matches no Dockerfile,
# docker-compose*, or .dockerignore path; the last commit to touch Dockerfile is
# bb89468 (2026-08-11); the PR's base 20e26221 passed its own Container Security
# run on 2026-08-13T00:05, ~25h earlier; and the scheduled run on 2026-08-12T08:08
# had already failed on main, so this class of failure predates the PR
# independently. The base did advance since the 2026-08-08 entry: digest
# sha256:5bbd41aef3ca86147ef389f3f0d944aa89bbae0d6c3ee31417e7bed15342aadf, with
# linux-libc-dev at 6.12.101-1+dhi0 rather than 6.12.100-1+dhi0.
#
# Independently corroborated against the Debian security tracker on 2026-08-14,
# each CVE fetched individually rather than inferred from scan absence, which
# suppression would have masked. All nine record source package "linux" as
# vulnerable in BOTH trixie (6.12.94-1) and trixie-security (6.12.101-1); all nine
# are fixed only in sid (7.1.8-1), which this base image does not track. That
# rules out the alternative remedy: a base-image digest refresh on the trixie
# track would ship the same nine, so re-pinning is not available here. The pinned
# GHCR digest above was confirmed still live (manifest HTTP 200) on the same date.
#
# The subsystems are KVM guest_memfd memslot binding (CVE-2026-64283), a libceph
# stack out-of-bounds write (68159), arbitrary code execution (68166), an ath6kl
# Wi-Fi use-after-free (68198), a drm/xe/pt current_op reset (68264), an idpf
# null-pointer dereference (68291), a BPF-redirect denial of service (68337), a
# mac80211 use-after-free (68409), and a stale skb->prev after async crypto in
# xfrm (68426). None is reachable from this workload: same kernel-UAPI-headers
# rationale as the blocks above, the package ships compile-time headers and no
# kernel binary, and the container runs a FastAPI application on whatever kernel
# the Docker host provides. Documented in docs/known-vulnerabilities.md; reassess
# 2026-09-30.
CVE-2026-64283
CVE-2026-68159
CVE-2026-68166
CVE-2026-68198
CVE-2026-68264
CVE-2026-68291
CVE-2026-68337
CVE-2026-68409
CVE-2026-68426
